{
  "result": "The first thing I ever found in a library book was a grocery list: eggs, lemons, something written so quickly it might have been either soap or soup. I was nine and had never considered that anyone could stop reading in the middle of a sentence to go buy eggs.\n\nAfter that I shook every book before I opened it. Mostly I got crumbs. Once, from a book about whales, a photograph of a man holding a birthday cake fell into my lap. The cake leaned to the left. The man was watching it instead of looking at the camera.\n\nI took the photograph to the librarian. She turned it over, but there was nothing on the back. Then she opened a drawer. Inside were two mittens, neither a match for the other, a playing card, and a key with a red cap. She put the man beside the key.\n\n\u201cHow will he know where to look?\u201d I asked. She said people usually came back for keys. Photographs were harder. I wanted to ask if we could put up a sign, but I couldn't think what it should say. HAVE YOU LOST A MAN? seemed likely to cause more trouble.\n\nLast week I found that same book about whales at a used bookstore. It wasn't the same copy; this one had a coffee ring on the cover. Still, I opened it to the page I remembered. A blue whale's heart lay there in cross section, big enough to climb inside.",
